  bx('bl', "Most reformers spoke <b>about</b> the oppressed; <b>Phule spoke for them and organised them</b>: first major reformer to address <b>caste, gender, class, economic exploitation at once</b>. Parallel: Shudras / Ati-Shudras and African American slaves both born into bondage, oppressor hid behind tradition and divine sanction."),
  tb(["Area", "What Phule did"], ["<b>Education</b>", "<b>1848</b> India's first girls' school, <b>Pune</b>, wife <b>Savitribai Phule</b> first female teacher (stones, dung thrown at her). <b>Memorandum to Hunter Commission 1882:</b> universal practical primary education, State funded + run, aimed at lower castes + girls, trained non-Brahmin teachers, mother tongue, nominal fees / scholarships, tighter inspection"], ["<b>Women</b>", "Widow remarriage; against child marriage; <b>1863 infanticide prevention centre</b> (anonymous deliveries for pregnant widows, shelter for sexual assault survivors, orphanage)"], ["<b>Dalit rights</b>", "<b>1873 Gulamgiri</b> (Slavery), Marathi: Shudras + Ati-Shudras = African American slaves, blames priestly class, dedicated to American abolitionists. <b>1873 Satyashodhak Samaj</b> (Truth-Seekers' Society) vs orthodoxy and caste; <b>Din Bandhu</b> newspaper <b>1877</b>"], ["<b>Peasants</b>", "<b>Cultivator's Whipcord 1881:</b> religion + bureaucracy + moneylenders, high LR, ritual expenses grind Shudra cultivators; festivals as instruments of extraction; self-reliance, land reforms, military labour for rural development; night schools"], ["<b>Workers</b>", "Night schools; co-founded <b>Bombay Millhands Association 1890</b> with <b>Narayan Lokhande</b> (main founder): early effort for industrial workers, not a formal trade union (no fees, rules, funds)"]),
  bx('ye', "<b>PYQ 1996, 2016:</b> Satya Shodhak Samaj = Jyotirao Phule, Pune, 1873, anti-caste movement in Maharashtra (status of Shudra + untouchables; no priest needed to reach God). Not Tilak (1996); not tribals in Bihar, temple entry in Gujarat, Punjab peasants (2016)."),
  sec("CHILD MARRIAGE " + P('2020'), 'r'),
  t("<b>Why so hard to uproot</b>", "<b>Explicit religious backing:</b> <b>Garbhadhan ceremony</b> = marriage before puberty, consummation at puberty; scripture itself sanctioned it, not a distortion", "Orthodox opposition stronger, more principled than on Sati (&quot;harder than Sati, therefore religious sanction&quot;)", w=22),
  t("<b>Legislative ladder</b>", "<b>IPC 1860</b> under <b>Canning 56-62</b> (Vidyasagar's efforts): minimum age of consummation <b>10</b> (<b>Section 375</b>, statutory rape): modest; Akshay Kumar Dutt added medical dimension", "Teacher: age 10 = &quot;not a reform but a law to prevent violation of customary Hindu law&quot; (Garbhadhan at puberty); did not raise marriage age", "Laws: Civil + Criminal; Civil: Personal (inheritance: by default personal law operates) + Non-personal. <b>Three at the border:</b> Sati 1829, IPC 1860 [age 10], Age of Consent 1891 [12]", w=24),
  t("<b>Rakhmabai case (1884-1888)</b>", "Married as infant, lived apart; at <b>22</b> husband (<b>Dadaji Bhikaji</b>) sued for <b>restitution of conjugal rights</b>; she argued marriage invalid", "<b>Rakhmabai Defence Committee</b> led by <b>Malabari</b> (his <b>1884 note</b> on child marriage + enforced widowhood caused a storm); orthodox Hindus backed husband", "<b>She lost</b> (post-1857 orthodox opinion strong); then <b>Queen Victoria overruled and dissolved the marriage</b>", "Later <b>qualified in medicine in England 1894</b>; among earliest female doctors with <b>Anandibai Joshi, Kadambini Ganguly</b>", w=24),
  bx('ye', "<b>PYQ 2020:</b> Rakhmabai Case 1884 = (a) Age of Consent debate and (b) Restitution of Conjugal Rights; <b>not</b> women's right to education. Answer (b) 2 and 3."),
  t("<b>Age of Consent Act 1891</b>", "<b>1890</b> death of <b>11-year-old Phulmoni</b> (abuse by husband); <b>Lansdowne 88-94</b> introduced Bill: age of consummation <b>12</b>; passed <b>March 1891, Act X of 1891</b>", t("<b>Debate</b>", "<b>Tilak:</b> British no right to interfere in custom + religion; reform from social movement, not colonial legislation", "<b>R.G. Bhandarkar</b> (Deccan College, Prarthana Samaj): research shows <b>Dharmashastras permit marriage after puberty</b> (&quot;Logic + Religion&quot;)"), "<b>Result:</b> failed on ground; <b>1900s census data</b> (every ten years from <b>1881</b>) showed child marriage continued across all castes", "Teacher: British &quot;not interested in initiating reform, may act if pressure&quot; (did minimum, age 12). Failure rule: <b>no mass movement, so most reforms failed</b>; UCC: reform not demanded from within fails", w=26),
  sec("FEMALE INFANTICIDE AND LEX LOCI ACT 1850", 'b'),
  t("<b>Female infanticide</b>", "North + west India, largely unabated; causes <b>structural:</b> landowning high-caste men practised <b>hypogamy</b> (handout) / <b>hypergamy</b> (board) = marrying lower-caste women, often several, so daughters' grooms very hard to find; <b>high dowry</b>", "<b>Female Infanticide Act 1870, Mayo 69-72</b>: law could not reach root", "Census: high <b>girl-child mortality</b> = not direct infanticide but <b>nutritional neglect</b>; cannot be legislated", "Teacher: cause = dowry + polygamy (UC husband, LC wife). Two problems: infanticide (infant girls killed): more L and E; girl mortality 1-5 yrs (malnutrition): more society, &quot;you cannot station a Havaldar in every home&quot;. Rule: until society changes, reform will not succeed", w=26),
  bx('ye', "<b>Spelling:</b> handout &quot;hypogamy&quot;, board &quot;hypergamy&quot; (higher-caste men marrying lower-caste women): learn the meaning."),
  t("<b>Lex Loci Act 1850</b> (= Caste Disabilities Removal Act 1850; handout timeline also lists a Religious Disabilities Act 1850)", "Protected <b>property + inheritance rights of converts</b>: before it, convert <b>forfeited ancestral property</b>; Act overrode; State stands for individual choice", t("<b>Two readings</b>", "&quot;Legal foundation of religious freedom in colonial India&quot; (contested): orthodox Hindus + WEMC saw State tilt to conversion", "Missionaries: long-overdue protection of converts from predatory disinheritance"), "Board links: 1857 Revolt, evangelicalism, fear of religion; orthodox Hindus and WEMC &quot;opposed&quot; it", w=26),
  sec("ISHWAR CHANDRA VIDYASAGAR (1820-1891) " + P('2005') + P('2021'), 'r'),
  t("<b>Vidyasagar</b>", "Born Ishwar Chandra Bandyopadhyay, <b>26 September 1820</b>; passed <b>Hindu Law Committee</b> exam; title <b>Vidyasagar</b> (ocean of knowledge) <b>1839</b>; prize money for school + rare Sanskrit texts", "Reason and rationalism supreme; &quot;I have no time to think about God&quot;; rejected other-worldliness; approach = Utilitarianism", "Tagore: <b>Father of Modern Bengali (Bangla) Prose</b>", "Champion of women's issues: education, widow remarriage, child marriage, infanticide", w=22),
  t("<b>The educator</b>", "<b>Sanskrit College, Calcutta:</b> Additional Secretary then Principal (<b>1851</b>, Dalhousie 48-56); threatened resignation unless lower castes admitted; added Western sciences, maths, English literature; wanted education in the <b>vernacular</b>", "<b>Inspector of Schools, Bengal (1856, Dalhousie 48-56):</b> <b>20 Model Schools</b>, ended physical punishment (Inspector = district; Director of Education = province, like SP and DGP)", "<b>Nov 1857 to May 1858</b> (mid-Revolt): <b>25 girls' schools</b>, <b>1,300 girls</b> at a time; books + transport free", "Female education = &quot;only true route to uplifting society&quot;", w=24),
  t("<b>Widow remarriage and polygamy</b>", "<b>1855</b> book on miseries of widowhood + petition with <b>1,000 signatures</b> → <b>Hindu Widow Remarriage Act, 26 July 1856</b>; first remarriage under it <b>7 December 1856</b>; he married his own son to a widow", "<b>1855</b> petition to Lieutenant Governor of Bengal with <b>25,000 signatures</b> against polygamy; two books against polygamy <b>1871, 1873</b>", w=22),
  bx('ye', "<b>Conflict (Act 1856):</b> handout + typed slide: &quot;under Dalhousie 48-56&quot;; board: &quot;passed during Canning 56-62&quot; and &quot;post in 1854 @ Charter 1853, by Dalhousie 48-56&quot;. Reading: groundwork Dalhousie 48-56, passed in Canning 56-62's year. For a code match follow the handout (Dalhousie 48-56); Canning 56-62 safe only if the question says &quot;passed&quot;."),
  bx('ye', "<b>Bethune School (PYQ 2005, 2021):</b> set up <b>1849</b> by <b>J.E. Drinkwater Bethune</b> (Law Member of Dalhousie's Council, President of Council of Education) under <b>grants-in-aid</b>; <b>Vidyasagar = Secretary</b> (appointed Dec 1850). PYQ 2005 statement &quot;Vidyasagar founded&quot; is false on strict facts (board: F, &quot;DRINKWATER&quot;) yet UPSC key (b) counts it true; PYQ 2021 asks Secretary = Vidyasagar. Bankim = first Calcutta University graduate: separate fact often paired."),
  sec("WIDOW REMARRIAGE: WHY IT FAILED", 'b'),
  t("<b>After the 1856 Act</b>", "<b>The catch: widow lost right to deceased husband's property</b>: law largely ineffective", "<b>Maharashtra:</b> <b>Vishnu Shastri Pandit</b> society <b>1866</b> → orthodox rivals at once set up a <b>counter-organisation</b>; <b>Phule</b> worked among lower castes but failed. <b>Madras:</b> Veeresalingam Pantulu society", t("<b>Pandita Ramabai</b> (rare exception)", "Brahmin woman, stayed unmarried long, married a lower-caste man; after his death studied medicine in <b>England</b>, <b>converted to Christianity</b> (criticised even by reformers), raised money in <b>USA</b> for a <b>widow home in Bombay</b>", "Opposed the new role model of the &quot;educated but obedient wife&quot;"), "<b>Failure:</b> post-1857 orthodox won the scripture debate; <b>even lower castes stopped</b> widow remarriage (&quot;Sanskritisation working in reverse&quot;, adopting upper-caste restrictions)", "<b>By 1900 only 38 widow remarriages in Maharashtra</b>; worked only in <b>Haryana</b> (already accepted)", "Teacher: pre-1857 reformers never went to masses, so orthodox took leadership: the vacuum second-wave reformers found", w=26),
  tb(["Year", "Law", "Who and what"], ["1829", "Sati abolition (Regulation XVII)", "Bentinck 28-35, via Roy; culpable homicide"], ["1850", "Lex Loci Act (+ Religious Disabilities Act 1850)", "Converts keep ancestral property"], ["1856", "Hindu Widow Remarriage Act (26 July)", "Dalhousie 48-56 (handout) / Canning 56-62's year (teacher); via Vidyasagar; widow lost husband's property"], ["1860", "IPC: age of consent 10 (figure: &quot;Age of Consent Act 1860&quot;)", "Canning 56-62; via Vidyasagar"], ["1870", "Female Infanticide Act", "Mayo 69-72"], ["1872", "Native (Brahmo) Marriages Act", "Mayo 69-72; inter-caste + widow marriage if couple leaves Hinduism"], ["1891", "Age of Consent Act (Act X)", "Lansdowne 88-94; age 12"], ["1929", "Sharda Act (Child Marriage Restraint)", "Age 14 girls, 18 boys; Harbilas Sarda (handout figure only)"]),
  bx('ye', "<b>Order trap:</b> handout one-liner runs &quot;Age of Consent 1860, Female Infanticide 1870, Native Marriages 1872, Age of Consent 1891, Sharda 1929&quot; but its flow-chart arrows run 1872 before 1870. Use dates: <b>1870, then 1872</b>."),
  sec("CHARACTER AND FAILURES OF EARLY REFORM " + P('2017'), 'r'),
  t("<b>What defined early reform</b>", "Led by <b>Western-educated upper castes</b>: anti-priest in orientation, upper-caste in composition", "<b>Primary focus women</b> (conviction + British used <b>status of women as benchmark of civilisation</b>; before 1800s criterion = language and literature; hence women's issues and mostly male reformers)", "<b>Scriptures central</b> (reformers, orthodox, even British: Bentinck 28-35's pre-condition)", "<b>Cultural nationalism:</b> modernity within Indian traditions", w=24),
  tb(["Why they failed", "Detail"], ["No mass movement", "Roy's complex Sanskritised Bengali prose; Prarthana Samaj only 72 members; small bodies, elite language"], ["Top-down", "British L + E authority, no demand from below; teacher's triangle: reform UC and LC follow"], ["British involvement bred distrust", "Fear religion under threat, pushed people to orthodox leaders"], ["Gradualism", "Slowed everything, orthodox consolidated"], ["No women in leadership", "No major reform body led by a woman; &quot;agent of change should be from the group you want to change&quot;"], ["Upper-caste leadership only", "Lower castes did not join movements led by beneficiaries of caste"], ["Silence on caste", "Central structural issue avoided"], ["Post-1857 shift", "Second wave found masses already captured by orthodox"], ["Rival orthodox bodies", "Dharma Sabha vs Brahmo Samaj (Sati); counter-society vs Vishnu Shastri Pandit (widow remarriage)"], ["Orthodox won scripture debate", "On widow remarriage; even lower castes followed"], ["Post-1875 Hindu Revivalism", "Positive face + negative face (communal tension, Hindu supremacy)"]),
  bx('bl', "<b>Hindu revivalism had two faces (teacher):</b> <b>+ve:</b> social reforms, cultural nationalism, no communalism: <b>SDS and SV</b> (Swami Dayanand Saraswati, Swami Vivekananda). <b>-ve:</b> &quot;only the Hindu God is the true God&quot;, aggressive reconversion (<b>Shuddhi</b>), aggressive <b>cow protection</b> → communal tensions."),
  bx('bl', "<b>Handout grid of reform organisations (Section 19):</b> Brahmo Samaj 1828, Young Bengal, Paramhansa Mandali 1849, Prarthana Samaj 1867, <b>Deoband 1867</b>, Satya Shodhak Samaj 1873, Arya Samaj 1875, <b>Theosophical Society 1875</b>, Aligarh 1875, <b>Ahmadiya 1889</b>, Ramakrishna Mission 1897, SNDP 1903, later <b>Self-Respect Movement 1925</b>. Deoband, Theosophical, Ahmadiya, Self-Respect appear only in the grid, not in class slides. <b>Adyar (Madras) = Theosophical Society HQ from 1882.</b>"),
  sec("THE ARYA SAMAJ 1875 " + P('2001'), 'r'),
  t("<b>Swami Dayanand Saraswati</b>", "Original name <b>Mool Shankar</b>, <b>Gujarat</b>; never married; <b>20 years</b> travelling; ended in <b>Mathura</b>: <b>Swami Virajananda</b> sent him to learn directly from the Vedas, gave him the task to spread Vedic knowledge and the name <b>Rishi Dayanand</b> (later Maharishi)", "<b>Vedas = ultimate infallible source</b>; rituals, idol worship, caste hierarchy, priestly domination = corruption: <b>Back to the Vedas</b>, not Western modernity", t("<b>Satyarth Prakash (1875)</b> (The Light of Meaning of Truth)", "Written deliberately in <b>Hindi</b> to reach masses; foundational text", "Attacks idol worship, polytheism, animal sacrifice, untouchability, child marriage, birth-based caste", "Critically examines <b>Christianity, Islam, Jainism, Buddhism</b>: Vedic Hinduism superior to all (board: but on ground, no communalism)", "Doctrine of Reason; claims <b>Vedas alone contain scientific truths</b>: Hinduism ahead, not backward"), w=24),
  t("<b>Arya Samaj (&quot;Society of the Nobles&quot;)</b>", "Founded <b>10 April 1875, Bombay</b>; motto <b>&quot;Krinvanto Vishvam Aryam&quot;</b> (Make this world noble); ten tenets (not imp): God, Vedas, dharma, truth, righteous conduct, physical + social good, love + justice, knowledge over ignorance, collective welfare, individual freedom within social rules", t("<b>What it did</b>", "Attacked idolatry, polytheism, priestcraft, rituals, birth-based caste, child marriage from <b>within the Vedic framework</b>", "Supported <b>widow remarriage, inter-caste marriage, female education</b> (&quot;an educated man needs an educated wife&quot;; widow-remarriage programmes <b>1880s</b>)", "<b>Caste:</b> discarded birth-based caste, denounced untouchability, upheld <b>Varnashram by profession, not birth</b> (Brahmans scholars, Kshatriyas protectors, Vaishyas traders, Shudras service; changeable, not hereditary, not hierarchical): meritocratic alternative", "<b>Shuddhi:</b> bring back those converted to Islam or Christianity; under Dayanand for those who came forward, not forced", "<b>Gurukuls</b> for Vedic knowledge + training spreaders", "Hinduism as a <b>&quot;religion of the book&quot;</b>: Vedas = <b>Shruti</b> (heard), <b>Apaurusheya</b> (not of human origin)", "<b>Swarajya</b> = freedom from foreign influence, cultural (&quot;CULTURE&quot;), not yet political"), w=24),
  t("<b>Successes and failures</b>", "<b>Failed in east and west</b> (competition: Brahmo Samaj of India 1866 in Bengal, Prarthana Samaj 1867 in Maharashtra); <b>succeeded most in Punjab + North-West Provinces</b>, esp. after <b>Dayanand's death 1883</b> (more popular, more aggressive). Why Punjab: intense religious competition (Hinduism, Sikhism, Islam, missionaries); assertive, organised, scriptural approach", "<b>DAV College Trust and Management Society:</b> first <b>DAV High School, Lahore, 1 June 1886</b>, headmaster <b>Lala Hans Raj</b> (Bhagat Singh studied there); teacher: <b>Western education + Vedas</b>", "Influenced (handout): <b>Lala Lajpat Rai, V.D. Savarkar, Madam Cama, Ram Prasad Bismil, M.G. Ranade, Madan Lal Dhingra, Subhas Chandra Bose</b>", w=24),
  bx('ye', "<b>PYQ 2001:</b> Arya Samaj founded <b>1875</b>, not 1835; Lala Lajpat Rai followed it; under <b>K. Hedgewar</b> RSS (1925) partly inspired by Arya Samaj's organisational model; Keshab's Brahmo Samaj for women's education and Vinoba's Sarvodaya Samaj for refugees true: answer (d). Teacher: contrast &quot;vs SDS&quot; with Vivekananda."),
  sec("REVIVALISM: POSITIVE AND NEGATIVE", 'b'),
  t("<b>Post-1857: two streams</b>", "<b>Positive:</b> confident assertion of Indian traditions, attack ills <b>from within</b>: Dayanand, Ramakrishna, Vivekananda", "<b>Negative</b> (after Dayanand's death <b>1883</b>): <b>Pandit Lekh Ram, Pandit Guru Dutt</b>, militant Arya Samaj, missionary-zeal Vedas, <b>aggressive Shuddhi</b> to reconvert from Islam, Sikhism, Christianity", t("<b>Provoked</b>", "<b>Singh Sabha</b> movement among Sikhs (board: 1873)", "Muslims: <b>Tanzim and Tabligh</b>", "<b>Cow Protection Movement</b> → <b>Cow Killing Riots 1893</b>"), w=22),
  sec("RAMAKRISHNA AND VIVEKANANDA", 'r'),
  t("<b>Ramakrishna Paramhansa</b>", "<b>Temple priest</b>, not a Western-educated reformer; <b>meditation, renunciation, monotheism</b>; Western ideas left him untouched; no criticism of rule but implicit rejection of Western values", "Appeal to educated Bengalis: <b>clerical jobs</b> under British = <b>Chakri</b> (grinding routine), spiritually hollow; reformism by 1870s felt like admitting Indians uncivilised", "Offered <b>not reform but spiritual richness of own tradition, undiluted</b>; Chakri left no time for spiritualism = source of dissatisfaction", "Teacher: <b>1833 UCS open to Indians</b> produced the clerks", w=24),
  t("<b>Swami Vivekananda</b>", "Ramakrishna's disciple: intellectual, activist, global; <b>genuine universalism:</b> a Christian becomes a better Christian by studying Vedanta; spread values, not label", "<b>Vs SDS</b> (Dayanand: Hinduism superior): Vivekananda believed in superiority of Hindu philosophy, <b>not a negative revivalist</b>; Upanishads: &quot;Spirit = curiosity = Q and A&quot; = Doctrine of Reason; admitted recent degeneration without blaming a religion", "<b>&quot;Service of Jiva is worship of Shiva&quot;:</b> social service is spiritual; <b>Daridra Narayan</b> (God in the poor); existing reform too elitist, go to masses through service", t("<b>Ramakrishna Mission 1897</b>", "Aims: spread <b>Vedanta</b> + social service", "Army of followers carrying Vedanta, Upanishads, Buddha, Jesus"), "<b>Akhadas + gymnasiums:</b> Indian masculinity = physical strength + mental spirituality; Indians not &quot;feminine&quot;, differently strong", "Accepted <b>idols + rituals</b> as aids to religious feeling (vs SDS): <b>far greater mass acceptance</b>", "<b>1893:</b> Western materialism + Indian spiritualism must come together; internationally famous", w=26),
  bx('pk', "<b>Mains 2019 close:</b> Netaji called Vivekananda the spiritual father of the movement in Bengal (answer skeleton)."),
  sec("BANKIM CHANDRA CHATTERJEE (1838-1894) " + P('1996') + P('2018') + P('2021'), 'g'),
  t("<b>Bankim</b>", "Without religious revival no revival of India; <b>Hindu secular outlook</b> (religion as cultural + nationalist force, not communal); <b>Krishna as modern politician</b>; rational Hinduism (board: &quot;Do Logic&quot;; &quot;values&quot;)", "<b>Anandamath (1882):</b> gave <b>Bharat Mata</b> + <b>Bande Mataram</b>, anthem of National Movement", "<b>First graduate of Calcutta University</b> (founded 1857; <b>Joddu Nath Bose</b> other of first two, <b>1858</b>)", "<b>Jana Gana Mana:</b> Rabindranath Tagore, Bengali; he translated it into English at <b>Madanapalle (Andhra Pradesh) 1919</b>, set to music there; distinct origin from Bande Mataram", w=22),
  bx('ye', "<b>PYQ 2021:</b> Madanapalle = Tagore translated Jana Gana Mana (1919); <b>not</b> Theosophical Society HQ (Adyar, Madras, from 1882); slide key: Margaret Cousins composed the tune; answer (c). <b>Novels (PYQ 1996, 2018):</b> Neel Darpan <b>1860</b> (Dinabandhu Mitra, indigo planters' tyranny) < Durgesh Nandini <b>1865</b> (Bankim, first Bengali novel; 1996 answer (b)) < Arya Samaj 1875 < Anandmath 1882; Satyendranath Tagore's ICS entry 1864 (ink) / 1863 (key) conflict. Rast Goftar, Maharatta, Nibandhamala = periodicals."),
  sec("SIR SYED AHMED KHAN (1817-1898)", 'b'),
  t("<b>Sir Syed</b>: most significant Muslim social reformer", "Social: supported <b>widow remarriage</b>; opposed <b>triple talaq, polygamy, purdah, child marriage</b>; wanted Western + female education; <b>modernisation without surrender of faith</b> (&quot;like RRR&quot;)", "Political: India = <b>summation of Quams</b>; Hindu Quam and Muslim Quam separate; unity between Quams but separateness; Muslim Quam (former rulers) deserved <b>special status</b>, Western education essential. Teacher: <b>&quot;Father of the 2-nation theory&quot;</b> (handout: planted 1870s-80s → Muslim League 1906 → Partition 1947)", t("<b>Institutions</b>", "<b>1875 Muhammadan Anglo-Oriental College, Aligarh</b> (later AMU): <b>Aligarh Movement</b>", "<b>1886 Mohammadan Educational Congress:</b> rival to INC (seen as Hindu)", "<b>Jamia Millia Islamia 1920:</b> breakaway faction at AMU, Aligarh; when Gandhi called students out of British-supported institutions in <b>NCM 1920-22</b>, AMU stayed pro-British; Jamia = anti-colonial, pro-independence"), "<b>Jamia founders:</b> Abdul Majid Khwaja, Maulana Mehmud Hasan, Maulana Md Ali, Hakim Ajmal Khan, Dr Mukhtar Ahmed Ansari (later headed All-Party Conference → <b>Nehru Report August 1928</b>)", w=26),
  sec("NARAYANA GURU (1856-1928)", 'g'),
  t("<b>Narayana Guru</b>", "Born <b>1856, Travancore</b>, <b>Ezhava</b> (lower caste; denied temple entry, public roads near upper-caste homes, education, jobs); wandering ascetic; Sanskrit, Tamil, Malayalam, <b>Advaita Vedanta</b> → caste philosophically incoherent", "Slogan: <b>&quot;One Religion, One Caste, One God for Mankind&quot;</b>", "<b>Institutional method</b> (like Phule): temples open to all castes; first <b>Aruvippuram 1888, Shiva idol</b>: &quot;This is not a Brahmin's Shiva. This is an Ezhava's Shiva.&quot; (no conversion, no legislation); temples, schools, ashrams; <b>SNDP 1903</b> (Shri Narayana Dharma Paripalana Yogam)", "<b>Gandhi visited Sivagiri 1925</b>: both opposed untouchability (Gandhi inside Congress, Guru from within community)", t("<b>Significance</b>", "Reform reaching <b>south India</b>, led by a lower-caste ascetic", "New method: <b>parallel institutions</b>, &quot;creating facts on the ground&quot;", "Held orthodoxy to its own highest standard"), w=24),
  bx('bl', "<b>Handout:</b> three responses to caste failure: Gandhi's assimilationist approach, Narayana Guru's reformist-institutionalist work in Advaita Vedanta, Ambedkar's exit through conversion to Buddhism."),
  bx('gr', "<b>Mains frames:</b> women's question 2017 (British yardstick of civilisation; Sati, child marriage, widows, infanticide, female education; scripture vs logic, Tilak vs Bhandarkar; laws top-down, most failed at scale). Phule 2025 (bottom-up on education, women, Dalits, peasants, workers). Young Bengal + Brahmo 2021 (reason alone failed, reason plus scripture worked; both elite, urban)."),
  sec("EDUCATION REFORMS: ORIENTALISM TO 1835", 'r'),
  t("<b>Why Western education came late</b>", "<b>Orientalism</b> (late 18th-early 19th c): govern Indians the Indian way, within Indian literature, languages, laws; &quot;reform could wait&quot;", "<b>Edmund Burke (1729-1797)</b>: critique of EIC led to <b>Pitt's India Act 1784</b>; Indians not ready for Western reforms", "Leading Indian Orientalists: <b>William Jones, Warren Hastings 73-85</b>; board: <b>1835</b> = date Western education became policy", w=22),
  t("<b>Key Orientalists</b> " + P('2018') + P('2021'), t("<b>William Jones</b> (not imp)", "Judge, Calcutta Supreme Court <b>1783-94</b>; mesmerised by Sanskrit, ranked Sanskrit epics above Greek, Roman, English", "With Hastings began <b>codification</b> of Hindu + Islamic laws into English (handout: <b>1775 Hindu, 1778 Islamic</b>)", "Founded <b>Asiatic Society of Bengal 1784</b> (Dwarkanath Tagore a member)"), t("<b>Warren Hastings 73-85</b>", "<b>Calcutta Madrasa 1781</b>: Arabic, Persian, Islamic law; train Indians to help rule &quot;the Indian way&quot;", "<b>Sanskrit College, Calcutta 1824</b> followed for Hindu law"), t("<b>Wellesley 98-05</b>", "<b>Fort William College, Calcutta 1800</b>: train newly arrived <b>British</b> civil servants in Indian languages, laws, customs; <b>William Carey</b> taught Bengali + Sanskrit"), "<b>Benaras Sanskrit College 1791: Jonathan Duncan</b>, not William Jones", w=24),
